{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-29379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/state.json" "file_sha256" "580c5be265b57a24fa0e632d6a886f25e44bd6784c9ba201b959839d7a6ccabc"} {"question" "q-29379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/d61528c7868baa92a1adbb2c4b4b23f5c975df80dbbabfeb449ad73acea97802.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $3$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/3a4e3b5a90900d204c3265187d01343233bcaa76d811276464742fa447e4543a.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/0936975d549f0f94f4c4139b9543142308c2cc56d38d1bba2c94dc6c681b3d99.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/state.json" "file_sha256" "580c5be265b57a24fa0e632d6a886f25e44bd6784c9ba201b959839d7a6ccabc"} {"question" "q-29379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "231dcb297761a37e11844447de9e6c09596393dfad2d670d32e4f2f1a33b171f"} {"question" "q-29379" "field" "selection" "attri