 of the following is the graph of $y={3}^{x+5}-4?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/state.json" "file_sha256" "580c5be265b57a24fa0e632d6a886f25e44bd6784c9ba201b959839d7a6ccabc"} {"question" "q-31468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/56f4446a00c12419f722dfd008e3cde785e759aa037fedd47911f0a783f4dfed.png)\n\n- To plot $y={3}^{x+5},$ we shift the graph of $y={3}^{x}$ horizontally by $5$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-5,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/871fe4053ba868f475ded7f0722ab7df20636a4db743f10c7631dede279715f1.png)\n\n- To plot $y={3}^{x+5}-4,$ we shift the graph of $y={3}^{x+5}$ vertically by $4$ units down. The point $(-5,1)$ moves to $(-5,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/3278d9e3e73ffee57ece6d0abf3343f8315c729e694f111dacef0e759dbe4ab4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/state.json" "file_sha256" "580c5be265b57a24fa0e632d6a886f25e44bd6784c9ba201b959839d7a6ccabc"} {"question" "q-31468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "231dcb297761a37e11844447de9e6c09596393dfad2d670d32e4f2f1a33b171f"} {"question" "q-31468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "fi